/* Generated by tools/export-vault-notes.py from the Obsidian vault. Re-run the script; do not edit by hand. */
Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"A change in price level produces the Income Effect; a change in net worth produces the Real Wealth Effect":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Income Effect and the Real Wealth Effect are two different economic concepts that, although somewhat related, describe different phenomena. The former is produced by a change in the price level, and the latter is produced via an increase in an individual’s net worth.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>These two concepts are very similar, but there are some crucial differences between the two that should be clarified.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The real-world applicability of the Pigou effect is contested\">The real-world applicability of the Pigou effect is contested</a><ul><li>The Pigou Effect is a particular type of real wealth effect whereby a general deflation of prices corresponds with a general increase in spending.</li></ul></li></ul>","A city is by nature a plurality":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A city is, by its nature, a conglomeration of reciprocal, free, and equal citizens who operate under the rule of law with a common goal in mind. However, this “common goal” should not be taken too strongly, since merely having an aggregate of similar people destroys the self-sufficiency of the city and reduces it to the level of household or an individual.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Aristotle’s views on the nature of plurality in cities, and it seems to be an application of its doctrine of the Golden Mean.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>This seems to me to be a very good heuristic for understanding a nation-state, as something that is ideologically and culturally diversified (e.g., left vs. right, religious differences), but where the ultimate aim of citizens are aligned (e.g., to create a democracy, ensure individual rights/freedoms).</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The city is the fundamental political unit\">The city is the fundamental political unit</a><ul><li>This note is important because of the city’s role as the fundamental political unit.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city\">The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city</a><ul><li>In fact, it seems to me that justice can only be applied between people who at least have some level of difference between them.</li></ul></li></ul>","A government's fiscal health and the health of banks are interlinked, and if one fails, the other likely will as well":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The health of the government economy is related to the health of the banking industry, and vice versa.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why government fiscal failures and bank failures are often correlated, and how the failure of one my instigate a failure of the other due to how tight the two entities are bound together.</p><h4>The Doom Loop</h4><p>There are two possible formulations of the doom loop:</p><ol><li>Due to rising government default risk, the value of their bonds plummets, causing the banks to lose massive value and stop lending. The government has to then bail-out the banks, meaning issuing more debt and devaluing their debt even more. At the same time, the economy enters recession due to the end of lending by banks, lowering tax revenue, making the government have to issue even more debt to spend.</li><li>The central bank raises interests rates, meaning that old government bonds become relatively more worthless. Banks retain these bonds, which are unrealized losses. This creates a delicate situations whereby when depositors rush to withdraw their funds, the bank is forced to sell their old bonds at a much lower price than initially expected, which may lead to the government having to bail them out, costing the government money, meaning that they have to issue more debt and thus devaluing more debt.</li></ol><p></p><h4>Empirical Data</h4><p>It has been shown that banks with higher sovereign exposure with a risky sovereign leads to a large fall in their stock value and higher Credit Default Swap spreads (meaning that they have to pay more to insurance their risk).</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Social Security contributes to the federal debt\">Social Security contributes to the federal debt</a><ul><li>An aspect of government spending that contributes to the federal debt, which could increase the chance for default.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The fiscal limit of a government is determined by the standard debt dynamics equation\">The fiscal limit of a government is determined by the standard debt dynamics equation</a><ul><li>This could be used to calculate the hypothetical debt limit of a country, something that is delated to this idea, since once the limit is surpassed, the country would almost certainly default.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Price level is driven by the ratio of government debt to expected future surplus\">Price level is driven by the ratio of government debt to expected future surplus</a><ul><li>This may be another related effect when government debt rises.</li></ul></li></ul>","A nation cannot permanently hold a stock of money that exceeds its relative productivity":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In a fixed exchanged rate system, a country cannot hold a stock of money that exceeds its relative productivity, since if a country were to hold more than their relative productivity, the money would simply leave and move to other countries through increased imports.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why although nations may seek to use the expansion of credit as a means of increasing consumption, this does not work.</p><h4>References</h4>","All things in the universe are manifestations of the mind and thus illusory and impermanent":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>All things that we believe exist in the universe are in fact only objects of the mind, and thus illusory and impermanent. Therefore, we must rid ourselves of worldly desire.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the central plank of Buddhism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The desires that people have manifest in <em>karma</em>, which causes one to be trapped in the eternal recurring cycle of existence (<em>samsara</em>). All the different schools of Buddhism are essentially trying to solve the fundamental problem of how to bring someone out of a worldly state and achieve <em>nirvana</em>, or enlightenment.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many aspects of Buddhism derive from Hinduism\">Many aspects of Buddhism derive from Hinduism</a><ul><li>Regarding the connection between the Buddhism and Hinduism.</li></ul></li></ul>","Basic desert holds that blame is warranted by the act alone and not by its consequences":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>On the basic desert conception, an agent deserves blame simply in virtue of having performed the act, independently of whether blaming produces any further good such as deterrence or reform.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the conception of desert that the free will debate is actually about, since consequentialist justifications of blame survive determinism untouched.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The conception carries a strong control requirement: the act must have been up to the agent in some robust sense, because otherwise there is nothing about the agent that the blame is tracking.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Some people must deserve something\">Some people must deserve something</a><ul><li>The pre-theoretical intuition that basic desert formalises.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Retributive punishment is never justified\">Retributive punishment is never justified</a><ul><li>Rejects the practical upshot of basic desert.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Disputes about free will are usually disputes about the nature of moral responsibility\">Disputes about free will are usually disputes about the nature of moral responsibility</a><ul><li>Why this notion sits at the centre of the debate.</li></ul></li></ul>","Business cycles arise due to artificial manipulations of the interest rate by the central bank":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The hypothesis that business cycles are the result of the central bank control of interest rate. Essentially, that the central bank control of the interest rate makes it so that interest rates are kept too low during booms, and too high during depressions, meaning that the effects of these interest rate changes are exasperated.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the Austrian explanation for the existence of the business cycle, and this explanation differs from the explanations for the business cycle offered by other schools of thought.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Savings and Loan Crisis was caused by interest rate shocks, risky investments, and regulatory failure\">The Savings and Loan Crisis was caused by interest rate shocks, risky investments, and regulatory failure</a><ul><li>The artificial manipulation of the interest by the central bank during this period of time could be fit as the factor that ultimately lead to the collapse of thrift banks.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Mainstream economics’ need for assumptions in their models justifies government intervention into the economy\">Mainstream economics’ need for assumptions in their models justifies government intervention into the economy</a><ul><li>Perhaps it is due to these interventions that bubbles are caused.</li></ul></li></ul><p>-</p>","China grows its influence with non-expansionary means":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>China has historically been a non-expansionary power, since in its view, it was the only true power around, and there was no civilization that could truly mount a threat against it. This trend is expected to continue being present in Chinese foreign policy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This theory could be used to predict the actions of the PRC.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Though it will not expand militarily, it will instead want to ensure that the nations around it</p><ul><li>Support its territorial integrity (with regards to Tibet, Taiwan, etc.).</li><li>To generate support for Chinese foreign policy.</li><li>To remain aligned with Chinese economic goals.</li><li>To support Chinese culture and Chinese people.</li></ul><p>Part of this diplomacy may be motivated by the fact that China’s foreign policy is continually tinted by its Confucian view, that of the harmony between different power differentials. In this way, China does not come off as outwardly aggressive, though in fact that is China’s stance on the issue, and it is made clear in more subtle ways.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Daoism argues for self-sufficiency and withdrawal from worldly affairs\">Daoism argues for self-sufficiency and withdrawal from worldly affairs</a><ul><li>This approach to foreign policy may be fundamentally rooted in Daoism.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China&#x27;s foreign policy is characterized by &#x27;theatrical diplomacy&#x27;\">China's foreign policy is characterized by 'theatrical diplomacy'</a><ul><li>This is one of the main ways by which China gains influence.</li></ul></li></ul>","Climate change poses an existential risk for humanity":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Climate change poses an existential risk for humanity in that it has the potential to reduce biodiversity, which can lead to ecosystem collapse.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the main reason that climate change should be avoided, though that are other, secondary reasons as well.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Another way of putting this is that through climate change, we lose access to ecosystem services, which are benefits provided by the environment (clean air, water purification, pollination, etc.).</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Protection from existential risks faces a tragedy of the commons problem\">Protection from existential risks faces a tragedy of the commons problem</a><ul><li>This is way climate change is so hard to solve.</li></ul></li></ul>","Daoism argues for self-sufficiency and withdrawal from worldly affairs":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Daoism argues for self-sufficiency and withdrawal from worldly affairs.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the core principles of Daoism, and is crucial for understanding its impact on Chinese culture.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>One should strive for simplicity in their living, abandon education and self-improvement, and be self-sustaining. Additionally, one should withdraw from worldly affairs.</li><li>While Daoists do want to make the world better, it does not seek to do so through direct action, as that would lead to artificiality and the prevalence of self-interest as a motivating factor.</li><li>Argues for \"non-action,\" or 无为.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Distributive Justice means that each individual receives the amount of something commensurate to their merit":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Distributive Justice concerns when things are being divided up. It states that each individual should receive the amount of something relative to their level of merit; otherwise, it is injustice.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Distributive Justice is one of the major types of justice that Aristotle discusses.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle expresses reciprocity\">The difference principle expresses reciprocity</a><ul><li>It seems that this idea directly contrasts with Rawls’ idea, since argues based on the idea that merit is inherent, and distribution is on top of that. Rawls argues that opposite.</li></ul></li></ul>","For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>For an action to be virtuous, not only does the action it self must qualify as a virtuous action, the intent/action behind that outcome likewise has to be virtuous.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important for understanding Aristotle’s definition of virtue, of what it means for someone to act in a virtuous manner.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>For the agent to have a quality of virtuousness, they must have the various moral virtues. For the agent to act in a virtuous state, they must:</p><ol><li>Act with knowledge of what they are doing.</li><li>Choose an action for its own sake.</li><li>Act with a fixed disposition, namely, that the actor’s virtue must be ingrained in themselves, and not just a fleeting impulse.</li></ol><p>Virtue is not determined by one’s emotions (the way that one feels about the world), nor by one’s faculties (the means by which one can feel emotions), but by dispositions (the way that we generally perceive the world that shapes the way that our faculties function).</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself\">Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself</a><ul><li>This echoes the idea that one has to act with a fixed disposition.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue divides into the intellectual, which is taught, and the moral, which is habituated\">Virtue divides into the intellectual, which is taught, and the moral, which is habituated</a><ul><li>This note refers specifically to moral virtue.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean\">To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean</a><ul><li>This refers to the consequence of the action, which must be in accordance with the golden mean.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions\">The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions</a><ul><li>Virtue is ultimately to achieve happiness.</li></ul></li></ul>","For regulators, taxes are generally more effective tools than bans":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>For regulators, taxes are generally better tools than bans, since they make markets more efficient and increase welfare.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This argues against the use of bans, which are common in policy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>If regulators want to ensure that the price of a product does not rise too high, they don’t have to simply ban firms from raising their taxes above a certain threshold. Rather a more sensible strategy would be to instead place a progressive tax on the revenue of firms that rise about the threshold. This has several advantages:</p><ul><li>This eliminates inefficient firms that have high marginal costs.</li><li>It preserves access for a good, instead of destroying it as in the case of bans.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The high tax rates of the 1950s were not actually paid, and resulted in rent-seeking behavior\">The high tax rates of the 1950s were not actually paid, and resulted in rent-seeking behavior</a><ul><li>At the same time, taxes might not work.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Inefficient taxes are a means of keeping the power of the government in check\">Inefficient taxes are a means of keeping the power of the government in check</a><ul><li>Tax can be used as more than an economic tool as well.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Under certain conditions, a wealth tax leads to greater investment in human capital and other intangible assets\">Under certain conditions, a wealth tax leads to greater investment in human capital and other intangible assets</a><ul><li>Another advantage of taxes.</li></ul></li></ul>","Implementing a minimum wage is ineffective for gig workers, since their supply is so elastic":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>It is ineffective to place a minimum wage on gig workers, since they supply is so elastic that it can adjust easily to any such practice.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This offers a critique of why minimum wages should not be implemented for gig workers.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>For instance, is a minimum wage is implemented for Uber drivers, because there are so many people with a car, the supply of driver would simply increase and there would be less work for each Uber driver, essentially keeping their overall net hourly wage the same. When the experiment was done in Seattle, the following was noted:</p><ul><li>Consumers tipped less.</li><li>Each full time diver experienced a -26% change in the number of monthly tasks, due to the decreased demand (from high prices) and more drivers.</li><li>All in all, the net change in the amount earned by the drivers was 0.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The minimum wage can reduce unemployment\">The minimum wage can reduce unemployment</a><ul><li>Not in this case.</li></ul></li></ul>","Labor productivity grows faster in manufacturing than service sectors":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Labor productivity grows faster in manufacturing than service sectors, since technological innovations lead to larger productivity gains when the sector is more capital-heavy (i.e., manufacturing).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is cause of Baumol’s cost disease, since it pushes up wages in relatively less productive sectors, putting a drag on economic growth.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Baumol&#x27;s Cost Disease slows economic growth due to increasing input costs in low productivity industries\">Baumol's Cost Disease slows economic growth due to increasing input costs in low productivity industries</a></li></ul>","Legal regulation is not context sensitive":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Any sort of regulation enforced by law is, by its nature, context-insensitive. For example, a law may prevent <em>x</em>, even though there might be (rare, but) generally acceptable conditions under which it would be moral permissible to <em>x</em>.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows the often unintended side effect of regulation, which can lead to a slew of unintended consequences, misallocated resources, and general harm.</p><h4>Aristotelian Political Theory</h4><p>Aristotle in fact brings up this point in his <em>Politics</em>, arguing that rule of law is context insensitive. In democracies, especially when the rule of law is weak, this can lead to the rise of demagogues who seek to bypass the rule of law and instead rule by decree.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Licensure is, under most circumstances, unnecessary and detrimental to society\">Licensure is, under most circumstances, unnecessary and detrimental to society</a><ul><li>A more specific claim regrading licensures and why they are generally detrimental.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents\">International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents</a><ul><li>A specific manifestation of legal regulation, therefore, directly benefit incumbents in a market.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy\">Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy</a><ul><li>If not legal regulation, then deregulation.</li></ul></li></ul>","Lot and his Daughters":"<h4>Notes</h4><p>The blue background and the use of sfumato lends the painting a unique quality seldom observed in other art works from the era.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Mater Dolorosa\">Mater Dolorosa</a><ul><li>Similar blue background.</li></ul></li></ul>","Lying is the root cause of immorality":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that lying, both to oneself and to others, is the source of immorality.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an idea that is present in various works of literature and philosophy.</p><h4><em>The Brothers Karamzov</em></h4><p>Elder Zosima says that lying is the root cause of evil since it means that the liar is no longer able to tell the truth everywhere, and thus loses respect for all others, including himself.</p><ul><li>Perhaps the claim about not being able to tell the truth lies in the fact that the liar has the prescience to the fact that everyone is (could be) lying, so he no longer trusts anyone, including those who actually speak the truth.<ul><li>Fyodor Pavlovich is the demonstration of this in practice. He has no respect for Elder Zossima.</li></ul></li></ul><h4><em>East of Eden</em></h4><ul><li>Lying is bad because it undermines the ability to see/believe in the truth.<ul><li>The lying to Aron about the nature of the mother, the lying to Adam and Charles by Cyrus, the lie of Cathy’s love to Adam (lying to himself).</li></ul></li><li>Ultimately, it leads to sin and bad outcomes.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Lying is a &#x27;formal wrongdoing&#x27; and not a material wrong\">Lying is a 'formal wrongdoing' and not a material wrong</a><ul><li>Lying as a formal wrongdoing seems to be the way that lying harms the world in many of the examples describes in his note.</li></ul></li></ul>","Music can influence your confidence":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Playing the right music can boost your confidence and charisma.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This fact could be used to achieve maximum confidence/charisma in the right situations. By playing music that makes me feel confident before an important event, I will be able to perform better.</p>","Non-price discriminating, monopolistic firms prefer technological innovations that cut costs rather than increase outputs":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Due to the fact that as one sells more, the price of a good goes down, firms that experience downward-sloping demand curves tend to prefer technological innovations that cut costs rather than innovations that increase outputl.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This describes a fact of firm behavior for monopolistic, non price discriminating firms.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An ordinary monopolist sets the price where the demand curve is unit elastic\">An ordinary monopolist sets the price where the demand curve is unit elastic</a><ul><li>They would not want to move the price below this point, for instance.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Monopolies should make more money\">Monopolies should make more money</a><ul><li>But if firms price discriminate, this would not be a concern?</li></ul></li></ul>","One ought no be held responsible if in not doing so, they act better, or if doing so produces more evil":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While usually one should be held responsible for their actions, they should not be held responsible when:</p><ol><li>by holding the person responsible, we end up producing more harm than good, or </li><li>the person is likely to act better while not compulsed by others.</li></ol><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains exceptions to the commonly held precept that people ought to be held responsible for their actions.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>The exceptions noted here are clearly utilitarian in basis. A deontologist would hardly accept either of these exceptions.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One’s initial moral sensitivity is irrelevant to whether one ought to be considered morally responsible\">One’s initial moral sensitivity is irrelevant to whether one ought to be considered morally responsible</a><ul><li>Aristotle would clearly disagree with the exceptions which are made here.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The congruence problem is easier solved by contract theories rather than utilitarian ones\">The congruence problem is easier solved by contract theories rather than utilitarian ones</a><ul><li>As seen here, the application of utilitarian theory produces from questionable results which feed into the congruence problem.</li></ul></li></ul>","Price level is driven by the ratio of government debt to expected future surplus":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This is the central idea of the Fiscal Theory of Price Level (FTPL), which emphasizes fiscal policy’s role in generating inflation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This presenting a contrasting theory to the Monetarist theory of inflation which attributes inflation solely to the amount of money in the economy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The FTPL is essentially represented by the following equation: $$ \\frac{B_{t}}{P_{t}}=Et \\sum (\\frac{S_{t+j}}{(1+r)^j}) $$ In other words, $$\\frac{\\text{Government Debt}}{\\text{Price Level}} = \\text{Expected Future Surpluses}$$ This means that a rise in the ratio of government debt to expected future surplus leads to a rise in the price level. The reason that this relation is true because when there is a positive difference between debt and EFS, the debt is not fully backed up by tax dollars, so people will spend more in order to convert their money into real goods before the value realizes its lack of backing, causing demand to rise and the price level to also rise. The opposite is also true. When there is greater EFS, people realize that there is more surplus than the amount of debt, they will spend less to prepare for the future taxes that will generate those surpluses, causing demand to drop and the price level to fall.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Price level is driven by the amount of money in the economy\">Price level is driven by the amount of money in the economy</a><ul><li>The competing Monetarist idea that the amount of money present in the economy is the sole determinant of the price level in the economy.</li></ul></li></ul>","Private property facilitates trade":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Without the concept of private property, no trade would happen. Trade is the fundamental driver of capitalism, as it is the prerequisite for the existence of markets.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Private property is the fundamental basis upon which capitalism is based.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself\">In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself</a></li></ul>","Pure procedural justice is the most practical form of justice to evaluate the basic structure with":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Pure procedural justice, since it does not involve judging the result against an independent criteria. Doing this would be impractical since the basic structure would produce too many individual distributions and transactions.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the reason that Rawls adopts pure procedural justice in analyzing the basic structure.</p><h4>References</h4>","The state is legitimized when it acts as a moral institution, and its head, a moral leader":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Confucianists, especially after Mencius explicated their theory of state in greater detail, saw the state as being a sole moral institution that grows naturally out of the hierarchies espoused by Confucian thought.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the way in which the state is justified under Confucian thought.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Since the leader of the state must be someone who a moral leader, only a sage can be a real king. Mencius traces this back to the sage Yao, and his descendants, who continue to be sages. Mencius in fact claims that if the ruler lacks the ethical qualities that make a good leader, the people have the moral right to revolution.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"People have a moral right to revolution when their rights are violated by the state\">People have a moral right to revolution when their rights are violated by the state</a><ul><li>This idea that gained most prominence during the Enlightenment in John Locke can be seen here.</li></ul></li></ul>","The theory of justice is in large part the theory of rational choice":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Justice is largely determined through the rational choice of individuals (from the perspective of contractarian frameworks), and thus, the study of justice is a study of rational choice fundamentally.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is helpful for understanding the foundation for many schools of thought in the contractarian camp.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This is derived from the fact that a theory of justice has to be:</p><ul><li>Logical and rationally justified.</li><li>Able to be agreed upon by agents.</li></ul><p>And a theory arrived upon via rational choice fits both these criteria.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice as fairness would be accepted by free, rational, and prudential persons\">Justice as fairness would be accepted by free, rational, and prudential persons</a><ul><li>Fundamentally, justice for fairness is a study of how agents rationally arrive upon the conclusion of justice as fairness.</li></ul></li></ul>","The virtue of prudence is concerned with how best to act in order to achieve virtue":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Prudence (<em>phronesis</em>) is the ability to deliberate rightly about what is good and advantageous for oneself (not what is good for something in particular, but what is good to the good life in general). Focuses on the action, not the production. It deals with the contingent/variable. Experience with particulars (through the virtue of intellect, <em>nous</em>) is required to become more prudent.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the most important intellectual virtues, since it is something that is practical and used everyday in order to rationally deliberate on what is needed to achieve the good life.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Understanding, good deliberation, and judgment are needed for the virtue of prudence\">Understanding, good deliberation, and judgment are needed for the virtue of prudence</a><ul><li>These minor intellectual virtues assist in making someone prudent.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state\">For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state</a><ul><li>The “acting with a quality of virtuousness” mentioned here is essentially acting with prudence.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Politics is the fullest realization of prudence, and it is divided into the legislative and political sciences\">Politics is the fullest realization of prudence, and it is divided into the legislative and political sciences</a><ul><li>Politics is the exercise of prudence in a polis.</li></ul></li></ul>","Tragedy can be more optimistic than comedy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that, though it may be unintuitive at first, comedy is a medium that can be far more pessimistic and negative than tragedy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an important interpretation of the forms of comedy and tragedy, reinterpreting them in a way that shifts and entirely changes their stated purposes.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>In tragedy, we take the noble aspiration of man seriously, in order for the tragic events to even have an impact. In comedy, however, we merely “laugh our heads off” when faced with the despairing reality of the world, and view the events of the world as simply petty and inconsequential. We withdraw from sympathy (as in tragedy) and engage in a more desperate and cynical act of laughing at the misfortune of others.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus the King (play)\">Oedipus the King (play)</a><ul><li>The story of Oedipus is used as an example of a tragedy when supporting the idea put forth herein.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus at Colonus\">Oedipus at Colonus</a><ul><li>Same as above.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Third Man\">The Third Man</a><ul><li>This is a counterexample to the idea presented in this note, since this “tragedy” is not in fact optimistic.</li></ul></li></ul>","Under a state of barbarism, typical legal & execution of moral norms no longer apply as they would under a sovereign state":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Under a state of barbarism, whereby there is power in the absence of freedom and law, no one is bound to typical legal norms as they would be under a sovereign state. Additionally, they are no longer bound by the typical execution of moral norms, meaning that although moral norms still apply, a state of barbarism may force immoral action in resistance.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why Kant would argue that you could lie to a Nazi officer if he came to look for hidden Jews. Since under the Nazi state, you would not be in a state of sovereignty, you are no longer bound by the typical execution of moral norms, meaning that you can lie justifiably.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Lying is a &#x27;formal wrongdoing&#x27; and not a material wrong\">Lying is a 'formal wrongdoing' and not a material wrong</a></li></ul>","Value is different from price":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that value is something that is different from price. Interestingly, this is both an idea in Austrian Economics, and in Marxist Economics, through the definition of value is different in both cases.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a core concept in both Austrian and Marxist economics.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>In Austrian Economics, value is subject and ordinal. Price is merely a reflection of the subjective value in terms of a currency. In Marxist Economics, value is objective and defined as the Socially Necessary Labor Time, which is the “average labor required under normal conditions to produce something.” This value is only observable through price, the chaotic signal on the market that fluctuates around the true value (SNLT).</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Labor Theory of Value is false\">The Labor Theory of Value is false</a><ul><li>A key part of the LTV is the idea of the Socially Necessary Labor Time, which is their definition of value.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Price level is driven by the amount of money in the economy\">Price level is driven by the amount of money in the economy</a><ul><li>Perhaps a reason that price is not representative of value is that the amount of money in the economy can differ, meaning that the price can change while the underlying value of something might not.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Price level is driven by the ratio of government debt to expected future surplus\">Price level is driven by the ratio of government debt to expected future surplus</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Price controls cause supply shortage, misallocation of resources, and rent-seeking\">Price controls cause supply shortage, misallocation of resources, and rent-seeking</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","Virtue should be shaped by law and public education if possible; otherwise, it should be shaped by family or friends":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle claims that the best case is that the state, since its laws have compulsory force, should shape the virtues of individual citizens. But if this is not possible, then family or friends should attempt to shape virtue.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the nature of how virtue ought to be instilled in the individual.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>Creation of virtue by family or friends could actually be good in a certain respect, in that they are more circumstantial/individual to the person that it is directed to, which could have a better effect than the blanket law of a state.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Politics is fundamentally a moral debate, not a factual one\">Politics is fundamentally a moral debate, not a factual one</a><ul><li>This is related to Aristotle’s idea here, placing politics as something that is moral in nature.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Natural virtue is a disposition towards virtue, but it is not virtue itself\">Natural virtue is a disposition towards virtue, but it is not virtue itself</a><ul><li>Natural virtue, plus the state or the family or friends, can create true virtue.</li></ul></li></ul>"});
